!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/40/io/ltr8/http/meta-service.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/net.tn"
@doc:"""
  A vocabulary for describing a service: what it can be asked to do (exchanges, grouped into protocols)
  and how HTTP exposes that (an http projection of endpoints). A schema importing this one is a service
  description, and `TsonApiDescription` reads it.

  The layer describes a service from the protocol's side, not the host language's. A protocol is a named
  set of exchanges, each one request-and-reply: a request record, a response record and error records. An
  http projection is HTTP's own constructs -- verbs, paths, headers, statuses, media types -- bridged to an
  exchange's request record by placement. A language is reached from that side, through the join
  (`design/invoke.md`), and no parameter list, return type or exception is ever written here.

  One vocabulary, two constructors -- and only two. `protocol` and `http` are the entries a description
  writes, so they are namespace types (§4.1): an entry applying one is a map from a name to a value, and a
  value, never a type. A protocol maps exchange names to exchanges; an http projection maps path templates
  to resources. Everything inside them -- `exchange`, `resource`, `endpoint`, `operation`, `binding`,
  `document`, `exemption` -- is an ordinary RECORD, because it only ever appears as a map value. A record
  composes (`operation => endpoint & signature`), is admitted as a subtype where its base is expected (§7.2),
  and is never a constructor anyone applies at entry level.

    orders => !protocol {
      @safe get_order => { request: order_ref  response: order  errors: [order_not_found] }
      place_order     => { request: new_order  response: order  errors: [sku_not_found] }
    }

    @binds:[orders]
    orders_http => !http {
      "/orders"      => { POST => !binding { exchange: orders.place_order  status: 201 } }
      "/orders/{id}" => { GET  => !binding { exchange: orders.get_order } }
    }

  Exchanges are keys inside their protocol, and endpoints are values two maps down -- keyed by path, then
  by verb -- so neither is a global entry: two protocols may both declare `get_order`, and an http
  projection needs no invented operation names. An exchange's qualified name is its protocol's entry, then
  its key.

  What an http projection claims about the protocols behind it is ANNOTATION on its entry, written before
  the name: `@binds` and `@not_bound`. A value of a namespace type is the map alone, with no fields beside
  its keys, so a fact about the projection as a whole has nowhere else to be written. A protocol lists its
  exchanges and nothing more; there is no inheritance between protocols, and a projection binding two names
  both.

  Three ways to use it:
    protocol only     a `protocol`; nothing HTTP in the document.
    web service only  an `http` of `!operation`s, each carrying its signature INLINE.
    both              a `protocol`, and an `http` that `@binds` it with `!binding`s naming its exchanges.
  An endpoint whose body is not a TSON value -- a schema document served as bytes, a page -- is a
  `!document`, listing its media types where the others state a response type.

  THE POSITIONAL FORM, AND WHAT IT COSTS. `resource` has exactly one bare REQUIRED field, so §5.6 lets
  the map stand in for it: `"/orders" => { GET => ... }` is `"/orders" => { endpoints: { GET => ... } }`,
  and the tag is optional under the path key. It reads far better and the examples use it. The cost is a
  versioning one and it is the reason this is written down: adding a SECOND required field to `resource`
  withdraws the positional form from it, and every document written in it stops parsing. So a new field
  there is optional or it is a new type.

  WHAT THE RESOLVER CHECKS, AND WHAT THE READER DOES. A relation that names something is a reference.
  A binding's `exchange` is a `meta.ref<exchange>` the resolver judges at schema load: a qualified name
  walking an entry and its key -- `orders.place_order` -- so a name nothing declares, one naming the wrong
  kind of thing, or an exchange key the protocol does not hold is refused there, and the qualified name
  says which protocol it means. The resolver does not judge a reference inside an annotation's value, so
  `@binds` and an exemption's `exchange` are typed as references here, saying what they mean, and
  `TsonApiDescription` checks them itself: each `@binds` name is an entry applying `protocol`, and each
  exemption names an exchange a bound protocol holds. What relates two things beyond naming one is the
  reader's too, when it reads a description, and an unsound description has no model: a binding's exchange
  belonging to a protocol the projection binds, the `@binds` claim, the placement of a request record over
  a path, a verb or a status against what the exchange declares, an error type fixing its status, a media
  type the server can speak, and no two endpoints sharing a verb and a path.
  """
!schema {
  @doc:"""
    The transport-neutral contract of one request-and-reply. A plain record on purpose: it is what
    `exchange` and an inline `operation` share.

    `request` absent means the exchange takes nothing; `response` absent means it returns nothing (an HTTP
    204). `errors` lists BUSINESS errors -- the request was schema-valid and the domain said no -- each
    a type composing `problem`. A transport's own refusals (a body failing its schema, an Accept it
    cannot satisfy) are not listed: every exchange has them and the error boundary answers them.
    """
  signature => {
    request?:         meta.type_ref
    response?:        meta.type_ref
    errors?:          [meta.type_ref]
  }

  @doc:"""
    An exchange: one request-and-reply, a signature and nothing more. Its NAME is the key it sits under in a
    protocol. A record, not a constructor: it is only ever a map value. The empty trailing body is what
    makes it a type of its own -- `exchange => signature` would be an alias, and an alias is a hop to its
    target, so the values would read back as signatures.
    """
  exchange => signature & {}

  @doc:"""
    A protocol: a named set of exchanges, keyed by name, and nothing else. A namespace type, so an entry
    applying it is the map of exchanges itself, and a qualified name enters it -- a `meta.ref<exchange>`
    reaches an exchange as `orders.place_order`, the entry and then the key. An exchange is a map VALUE under
    its name, so its name is scoped to the protocol, and annotations written before the key -- `@doc`,
    `@summary`, `@safe`, `@idempotent` -- document it and state its facts.
    """
  protocol => !meta.namespace { key_type: exchange_name  value_type: exchange }

  @doc:"RFC 9110's request methods (§9), called verbs so that `method` names nothing in this layer."
  http_verb => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  @doc:"Refined, so 42 is not a status."
  status_code => !integer ^ { min: 100  max: 599 }

  @doc:"""
    The naming roles of the namespaces this layer borrows -- one per namespace, as the kernel declares
    `meta.type_name`, `meta.field_name` and `meta.param_name` over `meta.identifier`. A key typed by a role
    enforces that namespace's grammar and names the namespace in the refusal ("'exchange_name': 'place
    order': U+0020 cannot appear in an identifier"), where `type_name` would misname the namespace doing the
    refusing. A namespace type's key is a name, so its role is an identifier family: where the authority's
    grammar is wider than TSON's identifier, the role is a `unicode.identifier_type` widened with `start_add`
    and `continue_add`, as `path_template` is. A map field's key may still be a text family carrying a
    `pattern`, as `header_name` is; a `pattern` is I-Regexp, matched against the whole token, so no anchors.

    An exchange's name: an identifier in its protocol's namespace, not a type name.
    """
  exchange_name => meta.identifier

  @doc:"""
    An http projection's key: an absolute path written as a URI template (RFC 6570) at levels 1 and 2, whose
    variables name fields of the endpoint's request record. `{id}` expands a value percent-encoded;
    `{+schemaPath}`, reserved expansion, leaves its `/` unencoded, which is what an identity path with
    slashes in it needs. The fragment operator is not admitted, since a server never receives a fragment,
    and nor are levels 3 and 4 -- several variables to an expression, query forms, prefixes and explode --
    which describe expansion rather than a route a server can match. A variable name is ASCII letters,
    digits and `_`.

    An identifier family, because it keys a namespace: it starts with `/`, and continues with identifier
    characters and RFC 3986's other path characters -- unreserved, sub-delimiters but `'`, `:`, `@`, a
    percent-encoded octet -- plus a template's braces and `+`. The `pattern` holds the structure the
    character set cannot: braces paired around one variable, and `%` followed by two hex digits. Being a
    name, a path key meets [TSON-DATA] §8.2's name hygiene too, so a mixed-script path is refused.
    """
  path_template => !unicode.identifier_type {
    start:        NONE
    start_add:    "/"
    continue_add: "/{}+%-._~!$&()*,;=:@"
    pattern:      "/([^%{}]|%[0-9A-Fa-f]{2}|\\{[+]?[A-Za-z0-9_]+\\})*"
  }

  @doc:"""
    A header field name: an RFC 9110 token, compared without case as RFC 9110 §5.1 has it -- held folded, so
    `Idempotency-Key` is `idempotency-key`, the form HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 put on the wire. It keys a map field
    rather than a namespace, so it is a text family with a `pattern`.
    """
  header_name => !unicode.text_type { pattern: "[!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+"  normalization: NFKC_CASEFOLD }

  @doc:"""
    Where a request record's fields go: the bridge from HTTP's constructs to the exchange's request record,
    and the place where a protocol and the http projection over it are allowed to look different. The verb
    and the path are not here: they are the keys an operation sits under, in its resource and its
    projection.

    An exchange has one request record, and the endpoint distributes that record's fields across the HTTP
    request: a `{segment}` in the resource's path binds the field of that name; `query` names fields
    carried as query parameters; `headers` maps a field to the header that carries it; `body` names ONE
    field carried as the whole body, unwrapped. Whatever is left is the remainder, and the remainder is
    the body -- unless the verb is GET or HEAD, or `body` already named a field, in which case it is the
    query. Every field lands in exactly one place, and a field carried in the path, the query or a
    header must be a scalar -- a URL segment cannot carry a record. That is what makes the mapping
    checkable: a field placed twice, a name the request does not declare, or a container in the path is
    a bad endpoint rather than a surprise.

    There is no parameter list. A parameter-only endpoint declares a one-field request record
    (`schema_ref => { schemaPath: text }`) and binds it from the path -- an exchange that takes anything
    takes exactly ONE record, which is what lets one rule serve every endpoint and the check see every
    field. `signature.request` is optional because an exchange may take nothing at all, and such an
    endpoint admits no placement: a `{segment}` on it is refused, having no field to bind. A request that is
    present and is not a record is refused too, since there would be nothing to distribute.
    """
  placement => {
    query?:   [meta.field_name]
    headers?: {meta.field_name => header_name}
    body?:    meta.field_name
  }

  @doc:"""
    What sits under a verb, under a path, in an http projection: the base record of `operation`, `binding`
    and `document`, carrying what each has -- the placement of a request record, the status of the response,
    and the media types the endpoint speaks. A value at an `endpoint`-typed slot is tagged with the subtype it
    is and admitted as that subtype (§7.2); a reader binds the base to a type with no bare form, so an
    untagged value is refused naming the subtypes. The tag is the whole difference between the
    one-declaration form and the two-declaration form, and it is read before anything else.

    `status` is the status of the success response; there is no list of responses, because an exchange has
    one response type. An error's status is the `status` member its own type pins --
    `sku_not_found => problem & { status: = 404  sku: text }` -- so an error type an endpoint names MUST fix
    it, and `TsonApiDescription` refuses one that does not. A transport's own refusals -- a body failing its
    schema, an Accept nothing satisfies -- are not listed: every endpoint has them, and the error boundary
    answers each with a `problem`.

    Every endpoint but a `document` speaks `application/tson`; `media_types` (net.tn's `media_type`) lists
    what it speaks beside it, absent meaning `application/tson` alone. One set serves both directions,
    request body and response; a split between what an endpoint accepts and what it produces is a later
    field, not this one. A label is listed, never implied: an endpoint answering JSON lists
    `application/json`, `application/tson+json` or both, and Accept chooses among what is listed. Media
    types, because the projection takes its vocabulary from HTTP's own registries (RFC 9110 §8.3.1), so a
    new encoding is a new registered name and never an edit to this layer. `TsonApiDescription` refuses at
    startup a media type no codec it serves can speak, so a description naming an encoding the server lacks
    fails to start and never answers 500.

    Documentation is annotation, not fields: `@doc` before the verb key is the long form and `@summary` the
    short one, both surviving into the read value as the key's annotations, since an endpoint is a map value
    and a map key carries annotations. A binding's exchange carries its own on its key in the protocol.

    Deliberately absent, each a decision: security schemes, response headers, links, callbacks, examples,
    tags, servers, and more than one success response. Adding one is a later version under §3.5 once this is
    published, never an edit -- and a new kind of endpoint is a new record composing this one, admitted
    wherever an `endpoint` is.
    """
  endpoint => placement & {
    status?:       status_code ~ 200
    media_types?:  [media_type]
  }

  @doc:"""
    An endpoint whose body is not a TSON value: a schema document served as the bytes it was published as, a
    page. Its `media_types` are the whole contract of its body -- usually one, `text/html` or the media type a
    schema document was published as -- and `TsonApiDescription` refuses a document listing none.
    `application/tson` is not implied for a document as it is for the other endpoint kinds. Its success body
    is not judged as a value, only labelled. It may still take a request record to place and name business
    errors.
    """
  document => endpoint & {
    request?:   meta.type_ref
    errors?:    [meta.type_ref]
  }

  @doc:"""
    The short form of an endpoint's or an exchange's documentation, written before its key --
    `@summary:"Place an order." POST => !binding { ... }`. Declared as the kernel declares `doc`;
    `@doc` remains the long form. Not a field, because documentation is metadata about a declaration rather
    than part of what it declares, and an annotation type declared here reaches every schema importing
    this one.
    """
  summary => @meta.annotation text

  @doc:"""
    Two facts about an exchange that a caller composing calls needs -- an agent planning a batch, deciding
    what may be retried or reordered -- and has no HTTP verb to read them from. Bare markers before the
    exchange's key: `@safe get_order => { ... }`, `@idempotent cancel_order => { ... }`. `@safe` implies
    `@idempotent`. Annotations rather than fields because they describe the exchange, not its request or
    response; the http projection CHECKS its verb against them (GET must be safe; PUT and DELETE
    idempotent) and never derives one from the other. They are RFC 9110's words for the same facts.
    """
  safe       => @meta.annotation void
  idempotent => @meta.annotation void

  @doc:"An endpoint with its signature INLINE -- the one-declaration form, for a projection with no protocol behind it."
  operation => endpoint & signature

  @doc:"""
    An endpoint whose signature is an EXCHANGE's -- the two-declaration form. `exchange` is a reference the
    resolver judges, its qualified name saying which protocol declares it (`orders.get_order`, the
    protocol's entry and then the exchange's key), so no binding is ambiguous between two protocols
    declaring one name. That the protocol is one the projection binds is the reader's check.
    """
  binding => endpoint & {
    exchange: meta.ref<exchange>
  }

  @doc:"""
    One path: its endpoints, keyed by verb. A record with one bare required field, so the map stands in
    for it and the tag is optional under the path key -- `"/orders" => { POST => !binding { ... } }` and
    `"/orders" => !resource { endpoints: { ... } }` read alike.
    """
  resource => {
    endpoints: {http_verb => endpoint}
  }

  @doc:"""
    Why a claimed exchange has no endpoint here: an exchange documented ahead of being built, or served by
    another process behind the same proxy. `reason` is required, because an exemption somebody has to justify
    in a greppable string is a decision where a boolean is a hole. `exchange` names exactly the exchange it
    covers, as a binding's does -- `orders.refund` -- so it can never quietly cover a second protocol's
    exchange of the same name. Typed `meta.ref<exchange>` for what it means; held inside an annotation's
    value, it is not judged by the resolver, so `TsonApiDescription` refuses one naming no exchange of a
    protocol the projection binds. A list of records rather than a map keyed by exchange: a map key is a
    name in the map's own key family, never a reference.
    """
  exemption => {
    exchange: meta.ref<exchange>
    reason:   text
  }

  @doc:"""
    An http projection: resources keyed by path template, each holding endpoints keyed by verb -- the shape
    OpenAPI's `paths` has, arrived at from the key types rather than copied. A namespace type, so an entry
    applying it is the map of paths itself. A path key is the URL namespace's own grammar -- a
    `path_template` -- which is why an endpoint needs no invented name and why `/orders/{id}` needs no
    identifier minted from it. Nothing references an endpoint, so no qualified name walks a path key.

    Named for the transport it projects a protocol onto, as a gRPC projection of the same protocol would be:
    it is one projection among several, not the description of the service. What it claims about the
    protocols behind it is written as annotations before its entry's name,
    `@binds:[orders] @not_bound:[{ exchange: orders.refund  reason: "..." }] orders_http => !http { ... }`,
    because a value of a namespace type is the map alone.
    """
  http => !meta.namespace { key_type: path_template  value_type: resource }

  @doc:"""
    The protocols an http projection binds, and a CLAIM the reader holds it to: every exchange of every
    protocol named has a `binding` naming it, or an `exemption` in `@not_bound` giving the reason it does
    not. The word is the one `!binding` and `@not_bound` use, since binding an exchange to HTTP is what the
    projection does with it. The claim is held per (protocol, exchange), not per name: two bound protocols
    may both declare `get_order`, and binding one of them says nothing about the other. A projection with no
    `@binds` is a web service on its own terms, its endpoints `!operation`s. Typed `meta.ref<protocol>` for
    what it means; held in an annotation's value, it is not judged by the resolver, so `TsonApiDescription`
    refuses a name that is no entry applying `protocol`.
    """
  binds => @meta.annotation [meta.ref<protocol>]

  @doc:"The exchanges an http projection's `@binds` claims and does not bind, each with the reason why."
  not_bound => @meta.annotation [exemption]
}
